WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.840
You already know how to describe motion along a straight line.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.500
Today we let motion turn corners, change observers, and leave the ground.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.020
Our tools are still position, velocity, acceleration, and time.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.380
We just need to keep direction in the picture.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.980
Choose east as positive x and north as positive y.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.060
A velocity with components three meters per second east and four north has

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.840
speed five, not seven.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.340
The components are perpendicular, so their squared values add.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.420
The direction is about fifty three degrees north of east.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.740
Components carry signs; the speed is the nonnegative magnitude.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.980
Now imagine watching a faster bus from a slower bus.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.060
The ground measures their velocities as eighteen and twelve meters per second

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:45.400
east.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.020
What speed does the slower bus see?

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.320
Take a moment.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.380
Six meters per second east.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.100
Relative position means the position of A minus the position of B.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.500
Differentiating that difference gives relative velocity: velocity of A minus

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.260
velocity of B.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.180
This subtraction works component by component, not just along a road.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.140
If the faster vehicle starts one hundred twenty meters behind the slower one

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.700
and both velocities stay constant, that gap closes at six meters per second.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.920
Catch up time is one hundred twenty divided by six, or twenty seconds.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.920
If B instead moves west at twelve, its signed velocity is negative twelve.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.700
Subtracting it gives thirty meters per second east.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.900
The familiar add the speeds shortcut is really signed vector subtraction.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.548
For two trains travelling east at eighteen and twelve, with lengths one

00:01:35.548 --> 00:01:35.900
hundred

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:40.160
and one hundred forty meters, complete overtaking from first overlap to last

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.120
overlap requires two hundred forty meters of relative travel.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.080
That takes forty seconds, assuming parallel tracks and constant velocities.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.820
A point catching a point and a whole train passing another train are different

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:53.740
geometry questions.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:58.800
In a JEE style problem, draw the initial separation before choosing what

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.480
distance to divide by relative speed.

00:02:00.768 --> 00:02:03.828
Let's take the same observer idea to a river.

00:02:04.628 --> 00:02:05.728
Here is our original example.

00:02:06.488 --> 00:02:09.908
The banks are one hundred twenty meters apart, measured northward.

00:02:10.988 --> 00:02:12.988
Water flows east at three meters per second.

00:02:13.748 --> 00:02:16.708
A boat can maintain five meters per second relative to the water.

00:02:17.828 --> 00:02:19.968
Ignore changes in current and the time taken to turn.

00:02:20.728 --> 00:02:25.148
The boat's ground velocity is its velocity relative to water plus the water's

00:02:25.148 --> 00:02:26.428
velocity relative to ground.

00:02:27.288 --> 00:02:31.568
Picture walking across a moving walkway: your own motion and the walkway's

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:32.848
motion both contribute.

00:02:34.168 --> 00:02:35.228
First, aim the boat due north.

00:02:36.008 --> 00:02:40.159
The ground velocity then has a north component of five and an east component

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:40.268
of

00:02:40.268 --> 00:02:40.568
three.

00:02:41.808 --> 00:02:45.048
Crossing time depends on the north component because that is the direction of

00:02:45.048 --> 00:02:45.728
the river's width.

00:02:45.728 --> 00:02:49.948
One hundred twenty divided by five gives twenty four seconds.

00:02:50.728 --> 00:02:54.148
During that time, the current carries the boat seventy two meters east.

00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:58.628
This is the minimum crossing time for the given boat speed and a current

00:02:58.628 --> 00:02:59.608
parallel to the banks.

00:03:00.608 --> 00:03:00.888
Why?

00:03:01.068 --> 00:03:05.008
The largest possible north component of a five meter per second velocity is

00:03:05.008 --> 00:03:05.388
five.

00:03:05.908 --> 00:03:08.608
Any upstream tilt makes that component smaller.

00:03:09.768 --> 00:03:10.588
Now change the goal.

00:03:11.148 --> 00:03:13.868
We want to reach the point directly opposite our starting point.

00:03:13.868 --> 00:03:15.648
Should we still aim north?

00:03:16.348 --> 00:03:17.668
Think about the current for a moment.

00:03:18.708 --> 00:03:18.828
No.

00:03:19.328 --> 00:03:22.559
Aim upstream so the boat's westward component relative to water is three

00:03:22.559 --> 00:03:22.828
meters

00:03:22.828 --> 00:03:23.348
per second.

00:03:24.108 --> 00:03:25.908
It cancels the current's eastward three.

00:03:26.328 --> 00:03:31.008
The boat's own speed is still five, so its north component is the square root

00:03:31.008 --> 00:03:34.028
of twenty five minus nine: four meters per second.

00:03:34.828 --> 00:03:36.548
The crossing now takes thirty seconds.

00:03:37.308 --> 00:03:40.874
The upstream heading makes about thirty seven degrees with north, since sine

00:03:40.874 --> 00:03:40.968
of

00:03:40.968 --> 00:03:42.768
that angle is three divided by five.

00:03:43.608 --> 00:03:47.068
Minimum time and zero drift are different instructions, not competing answers

00:03:47.068 --> 00:03:47.948
to the same question.

00:03:48.608 --> 00:03:52.384
In this ideal model, directly opposite landing requires boat speed greater

00:03:52.384 --> 00:03:52.588
than

00:03:52.588 --> 00:03:53.088
current speed.

00:03:53.888 --> 00:03:57.768
Equal speeds leave no north component when the current is canceled.

00:03:57.768 --> 00:03:59.816
Read the requested destination before calculating, and always label the frame

00:03:59.816 --> 00:03:59.928
of each velocity.

00:03:59.928 --> 00:04:04.048
A ball follows a curved path, but our equations do not have to become

00:04:04.048 --> 00:04:04.628
mysterious.

00:04:05.668 --> 00:04:08.208
Split its motion into horizontal and vertical components.

00:04:09.288 --> 00:04:13.888
We assume a uniform downward gravitational field and neglect air resistance.

00:04:15.228 --> 00:04:17.888
After launch, gravity is the only acceleration.

00:04:18.888 --> 00:04:23.348
Choose the launch point as origin, positive x horizontally, and positive y

00:04:23.348 --> 00:04:23.688
upward.

00:04:24.408 --> 00:04:28.788
For speed u at angle theta above the horizontal, the initial horizontal

00:04:28.788 --> 00:04:31.148
component is u times cosine theta.

00:04:32.248 --> 00:04:34.628
The vertical component is u times sine theta.

00:04:35.648 --> 00:04:39.168
Horizontal acceleration is zero, so horizontal velocity stays constant.

00:04:40.448 --> 00:04:43.868
Vertical acceleration is negative g, so vertical velocity changes.

00:04:44.808 --> 00:04:46.688
Both directions share the same elapsed time.

00:04:46.748 --> 00:04:48.068
That is what connects them.

00:04:48.928 --> 00:04:52.503
Horizontal displacement is the initial horizontal component multiplied by

00:04:52.503 --> 00:04:52.748
time.

00:04:53.348 --> 00:04:57.248
Vertical displacement is the initial vertical component multiplied by time,

00:04:57.708 --> 00:04:59.608
minus one half g times time squared.

00:05:00.528 --> 00:05:04.328
Let's launch an ideal ball at twenty meters per second, thirty degrees above

00:05:04.328 --> 00:05:04.748
horizontal.

00:05:05.648 --> 00:05:08.688
Use g equal to ten meters per second squared for this example.

00:05:09.488 --> 00:05:13.288
Its horizontal component is ten square root of three, and its vertical

00:05:13.288 --> 00:05:14.348
component is ten.

00:05:15.128 --> 00:05:17.648
At the top, vertical velocity becomes zero.

00:05:18.468 --> 00:05:22.288
Ten minus ten times t equals zero, giving one second to reach the top.

00:05:22.928 --> 00:05:23.648
Has the ball stopped?

00:05:24.128 --> 00:05:24.668
Take a moment.

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:26.948
Only its vertical component has stopped.

00:05:27.528 --> 00:05:30.708
Its horizontal velocity is still ten square root of three.

00:05:31.428 --> 00:05:32.908
Acceleration is still ten downward.

00:05:33.608 --> 00:05:38.208
The maximum height above launch is ten squared divided by twice ten, or five

00:05:38.208 --> 00:05:38.468
meters.

00:05:39.068 --> 00:05:43.368
If the ball lands at its launch height, the nonzero root of zero equals ten t

00:05:43.368 --> 00:05:45.608
minus five t squared is two seconds.

00:05:46.568 --> 00:05:50.308
Multiply that time by the horizontal component to get a range of twenty square

00:05:50.308 --> 00:05:52.768
root of three meters, about thirty four point six.

00:05:53.668 --> 00:05:56.328
Eliminating time gives a downward opening parabolic trajectory.

00:05:57.148 --> 00:06:00.711
The curve comes from horizontal motion proportional to time and vertical

00:06:00.711 --> 00:06:01.008
motion

00:06:01.008 --> 00:06:02.068
containing time squared.

00:06:02.728 --> 00:06:07.651
For same height landing, time of flight is twice u sine theta divided by g,

00:06:07.651 --> 00:06:07.848
and

00:06:07.848 --> 00:06:10.728
range is u squared sine twice theta divided by g.

00:06:11.468 --> 00:06:15.028
Those shortcuts require the stated ideal model and equal launch and landing

00:06:15.028 --> 00:06:15.268
heights.

00:06:15.848 --> 00:06:17.688
They are not universal projectile formulas.

00:06:18.048 --> 00:06:20.568
Now the landing point is below the launch point.

00:06:21.328 --> 00:06:24.914
This is where memorized same height formulas can quietly give the wrong

00:06:24.914 --> 00:06:25.268
answer.

00:06:26.508 --> 00:06:30.618
Imagine an ideal horizontal launch at fifteen meters per second from a

00:06:30.618 --> 00:06:31.088
platform

00:06:31.088 --> 00:06:33.228
twenty meters above the landing surface.

00:06:34.288 --> 00:06:36.768
Use g equal to ten and ignore air resistance.

00:06:37.848 --> 00:06:40.348
Put the origin at launch and keep upward positive.

00:06:41.168 --> 00:06:42.768
Initial vertical velocity is zero.

00:06:43.528 --> 00:06:47.968
At landing, y is negative twenty, not positive twenty.

00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:52.168
Our vertical equation becomes negative twenty equals negative five t squared.

00:06:53.088 --> 00:06:57.688
Time is two seconds; discard the negative root because we are asking about the

00:06:57.688 --> 00:06:58.428
event after launch.

00:06:59.688 --> 00:07:02.768
Horizontal displacement is fifteen times two, or thirty meters.

00:07:03.788 --> 00:07:07.768
Notice why increasing horizontal launch speed would increase range without

00:07:07.768 --> 00:07:09.648
changing falling time in this ideal model.

00:07:10.488 --> 00:07:14.088
The vertical drop and vertical initial velocity have not changed.

00:07:15.008 --> 00:07:19.208
At impact, horizontal velocity is still positive fifteen.

00:07:19.208 --> 00:07:21.728
Vertical velocity is negative twenty.

00:07:21.728 --> 00:07:25.848
The speed is the square root of fifteen squared plus twenty squared, giving

00:07:25.848 --> 00:07:27.368
twenty five meters per second.

00:07:28.248 --> 00:07:31.188
The direction is about fifty three degrees below horizontal.

00:07:31.648 --> 00:07:34.828
We do not add fifteen and twenty as scalar speeds.

00:07:35.528 --> 00:07:36.908
Those components are perpendicular.

00:07:37.788 --> 00:07:41.908
For an angled launch from a height, use y equals initial vertical velocity

00:07:41.908 --> 00:07:46.928
times t minus one half g t squared, and set y to the actual signed landing

00:07:46.928 --> 00:07:47.228
height.

00:07:48.028 --> 00:07:51.734
A quadratic can have two positive roots if the projectile passes a raised

00:07:51.734 --> 00:07:51.988
level

00:07:51.988 --> 00:07:53.808
once upward and again downward.

00:07:54.608 --> 00:07:56.948
The situation tells you which event is requested.

00:07:57.828 --> 00:08:01.848
There is one useful range result worth understanding: at fixed launch speed,

00:08:02.168 --> 00:08:06.208
with the same launch and landing height, range depends on sine of twice the

00:08:06.208 --> 00:08:06.648
launch angle.

00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:09.488
It is largest at forty five degrees.

00:08:10.308 --> 00:08:13.670
Complementary angles have equal ranges but generally different flight times

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:14.208
and heights.

00:08:15.008 --> 00:08:19.008
For different height landing, neither that equal range symmetry nor forty five

00:08:19.008 --> 00:08:21.248
degrees as the optimum is generally valid.

00:08:22.028 --> 00:08:25.148
In JEE practice, first write the physical landing condition.

00:08:25.828 --> 00:08:29.228
Let the equation choose the time instead of making the scene fit a remembered

00:08:29.228 --> 00:08:29.648
shortcut.

00:08:29.952 --> 00:08:33.332
Constant speed does not always mean zero acceleration.

00:08:34.092 --> 00:08:35.672
Watch a point moving around a circle.

00:08:36.032 --> 00:08:40.092
At the rightmost point, an anticlockwise moving point has upward velocity.

00:08:40.872 --> 00:08:42.992
A little later, its velocity tilts left.

00:08:43.592 --> 00:08:47.032
The speed can stay the same while the velocity vector changes direction.

00:08:47.712 --> 00:08:49.592
That change requires acceleration.

00:08:50.452 --> 00:08:54.592
For uniform circular motion, acceleration points toward the center, while

00:08:54.592 --> 00:08:56.272
velocity is tangent to the path.

00:08:56.872 --> 00:09:00.812
Over a very short interval, the little triangle formed by the velocity change

00:09:00.812 --> 00:09:04.252
is similar to the triangle formed by two nearby radius vectors.

00:09:05.092 --> 00:09:09.232
The magnitude of the velocity change divided by speed is approximately the

00:09:09.232 --> 00:09:11.232
traveled arc length divided by radius.

00:09:12.012 --> 00:09:14.272
Divide by the short time interval and take its limit.

00:09:14.892 --> 00:09:18.352
The inward acceleration becomes speed squared divided by radius.

00:09:18.912 --> 00:09:23.214
This is a kinematic result; a later force analysis identifies which real

00:09:23.214 --> 00:09:23.572
forces

00:09:23.572 --> 00:09:24.152
supply it.

00:09:24.712 --> 00:09:28.172
Angular speed omega means angle turned per second in radians.

00:09:29.092 --> 00:09:33.132
Arc length equals radius times angle, so speed equals omega times radius.

00:09:33.892 --> 00:09:38.232
One revolution covers two pi radians and takes a period of two pi divided by

00:09:38.232 --> 00:09:38.412
omega.

00:09:39.232 --> 00:09:42.392
Suppose radius is two meters and speed is four meters per second.

00:09:43.092 --> 00:09:47.572
Omega is two radians per second, period is pi seconds, and inward acceleration

00:09:47.572 --> 00:09:49.212
is eight meters per second squared.

00:09:49.992 --> 00:09:54.272
After half a revolution, distance traveled is two pi meters, but displacement

00:09:54.272 --> 00:09:56.372
magnitude is the diameter, four meters.

00:09:57.252 --> 00:09:58.472
Average speed remains four.

00:09:59.032 --> 00:10:02.832
Average velocity magnitude is displacement divided by half period: eight

00:10:02.832 --> 00:10:04.572
divided by pi meters per second.

00:10:05.292 --> 00:10:06.512
Now let the point speed up.

00:10:06.972 --> 00:10:10.572
It still needs radial acceleration to turn, and it also has tangential

00:10:10.572 --> 00:10:12.012
acceleration to change speed.

00:10:12.832 --> 00:10:17.312
At the instant when speed is four and radius is two, radial acceleration is

00:10:17.312 --> 00:10:17.612
eight.

00:10:18.192 --> 00:10:22.812
If tangential acceleration is six, total acceleration magnitude is ten, from

00:10:22.812 --> 00:10:25.832
the square root of sixty four plus thirty six.

00:10:25.832 --> 00:10:29.812
The two components are perpendicular; their scalar magnitudes must not simply

00:10:29.812 --> 00:10:30.432
be added.

00:10:31.072 --> 00:10:33.592
Always use instantaneous speed in the radial formula.

00:10:34.572 --> 00:10:38.552
Uniform circular motion has constant angular speed, not constant velocity.

00:10:38.952 --> 00:10:41.472
That distinction is a common conceptual test.

00:10:41.664 --> 00:10:46.324
Let's finish with a problem that refuses to use constant acceleration.

00:10:47.104 --> 00:10:52.464
A particle's position in meters is t cubed minus six t squared plus nine t,

00:10:52.464 --> 00:10:53.564
with time in seconds.

00:10:54.504 --> 00:10:57.464
The numerical coefficients carry the corresponding SI units.

00:10:58.364 --> 00:11:00.264
We study time from zero to three seconds.

00:11:01.064 --> 00:11:04.824
Instantaneous velocity is the slope of the position time graph, so

00:11:04.824 --> 00:11:05.824
differentiate position.

00:11:07.024 --> 00:11:10.064
Velocity is three t squared minus twelve t plus nine.

00:11:10.644 --> 00:11:15.204
Differentiate again: acceleration is six t minus twelve.

00:11:15.204 --> 00:11:18.604
Because acceleration changes with time, do not use the constant acceleration

00:11:18.604 --> 00:11:19.984
equations over the whole journey.

00:11:20.844 --> 00:11:24.464
Factor the velocity as three times t minus one times t minus three.

00:11:25.424 --> 00:11:28.984
It is positive from zero to one second and negative from one to three.

00:11:30.004 --> 00:11:31.824
The particle turns around at one second.

00:11:32.344 --> 00:11:35.944
At three seconds it reaches zero velocity again, at our interval's endpoint.

00:11:36.744 --> 00:11:40.324
Now calculate position at the boundaries and at the interior turning point.

00:11:41.504 --> 00:11:42.604
Position at zero is zero.

00:11:43.644 --> 00:11:44.844
Position at one is four meters.

00:11:45.944 --> 00:11:47.324
Position at three is zero again.

00:11:48.264 --> 00:11:49.304
What is the total distance?

00:11:49.944 --> 00:11:51.664
Take a moment to separate it from displacement.

00:11:52.644 --> 00:11:57.304
The particle goes from zero to four, then from four back to zero: eight meters

00:11:57.304 --> 00:11:57.724
traveled.

00:11:58.784 --> 00:11:59.484
Displacement is zero.

00:12:00.104 --> 00:12:04.144
Its average velocity over three seconds is zero, but average speed is eight

00:12:04.144 --> 00:12:05.724
divided by three meters per second.

00:12:07.064 --> 00:12:11.141
The positive and negative areas under the velocity time curve are plus four

00:12:11.141 --> 00:12:11.304
and

00:12:11.304 --> 00:12:12.064
minus four meters.

00:12:12.964 --> 00:12:15.204
That signed area check agrees with the positions.

00:12:16.064 --> 00:12:20.179
At two seconds, acceleration is zero, but velocity is negative three meters

00:12:20.179 --> 00:12:20.344
per

00:12:20.344 --> 00:12:20.584
second.

00:12:21.484 --> 00:12:23.884
Zero acceleration at an instant does not mean rest.

00:12:24.644 --> 00:12:28.644
Before that instant, velocity and acceleration are both negative, so speed

00:12:28.644 --> 00:12:29.104
increases.

00:12:29.104 --> 00:12:34.024
After it, velocity is negative while acceleration is positive, so speed

00:12:34.024 --> 00:12:34.564
decreases.

00:12:35.524 --> 00:12:39.684
For a position graph, slope gives velocity; for a velocity graph, slope gives

00:12:39.684 --> 00:12:42.084
acceleration and signed area gives displacement.

00:12:43.044 --> 00:12:46.564
For an acceleration graph, signed area gives change in velocity, not

00:12:46.564 --> 00:12:47.024
displacement.

00:12:47.944 --> 00:12:50.693
This class adds the main missing motion models to our straight line

00:12:50.693 --> 00:12:51.144
foundation.

00:12:51.944 --> 00:12:55.904
More multi stage projectile, relative motion and calculus practice still

00:12:55.904 --> 00:12:56.684
belongs in revision.

00:12:57.284 --> 00:13:01.284
Choose the frame, identify the acceleration model, solve the event condition,

00:13:01.644 --> 00:13:02.584
and then check the graph.

00:13:03.324 --> 00:13:06.312
That routine is far more dependable than hunting for a formula that merely

00:13:06.312 --> 00:13:06.480
looks familiar.
